Interpretation
--------------
cp5 noise floor : 1.257 µV RMS — within 11% of the 2026-05-22 baseline
                  (1.416 µV). Rig stability confirmed.

cp6 mini-sweep  : POWER readback 0x13 confirms VBIAS bit landed.
                  All six gain points PASS.
                  Output-referred RMS stays in single-digit µV at all
                  gains. Input-referred RMS drops monotonically from
                  1.314 µV (gain=1) to 0.121 µV (gain=32) — textbook
                  PGA-improves-resolution behavior.

OFFSET PATTERN  : Output-referred mean is approximately constant at
                  ~740 µV across all gains, NOT input-referred mean as
                  the README's illustrative example predicted. This
                  means the chip's residual offset is dominated by
                  POST-PGA backend offset, not input-referred offset.
                  Pattern signature: in_mean = out_mean / gain (clean
                  1/gain falloff: 740 → 370 → 185 → 92 → 47 → 23 µV).
                  This is a valid healthy pattern — the chip is fine;
                  the README's expected-pattern wording is what needs
                  updating.


Issues encountered and resolved during this bring-up session
------------------------------------------------------------
1. DFU UPLOAD CONFUSION — after a fresh flash the H7 entered a
   fast-blink green state and would not enumerate. Recovery procedure
   that worked:
   a) Double-tap reset on the H7 module (not Mid Carrier) to enter
      persistent bootloader (slow green fade, ~2 s period).
   b) `pio run -t upload` failed initially with "Cannot configure
      port, PermissionError(13)" when COM7 was still enumerated.
   c) Once DFU mode was clean (no COM port), flash succeeded.

   For future reference: in DFU mode the H7 does NOT enumerate as a
   COM port — it enumerates as a USB DFU device. If COM7 (or any
   COM port) is visible while you expect DFU mode, the board has
   left DFU and you need to re-trigger the double-tap.

2. OBSOLETE PIO CORE WARNING — PIO printed "Obsolete PIO Core
   v6.1.16 is used (previous was 6.1.18)" at the top of every run.
   Indicates two PIO installations are fighting on the system.
   Did not block the flash but should be cleaned up — see
   https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/core/installation/troubleshooting.html
